Nothing ruins a yoga intensive faster than acute gastroenteritis. "Bali Belly" is typically caused by ingesting bacteria (like E. coli) from untreated tap water, contaminated ice, or unwashed raw salad leaves at cheap street warungs.

When you are traveling alone in a foreign country, getting sick in bed can feel terrifying. Here is how we prevent this entirely inside our sanctuary:

Nourishing organic vegetarian meals shared by yoga students at Bali YTTC
Fresh, farm-to-table Sattvic nutrition prepared with 100% UV triple-filtered water.

100% Triple-Filtered Water System: Tap water in Bali is not drinkable. At Bali YTTC, all water used in our kitchens—for cooking rice, washing salad greens, boiling tea, and ice—passes through a professional triple-filtration and UV purification system. Students have unlimited access to sanitized dispenser stations to refill their stainless steel water bottles.
Farm-to-Table Sattvic Cooking: Three freshly cooked vegetarian and vegan meals are served daily. Meals are prepared according to Ayurvedic principles: lightly spiced, easy on digestion, rich in fiber, and cooked fresh two hours before serving. No reheated buffets sitting out under the tropical sun.
Proactive Gut Support: We start every morning with warm fresh ginger, turmeric, and lemongrass infusions that naturally stimulate digestion and maintain strong gut flora.
Emergency Support Protocol: If a student ever feels unwell, our on-site team steps in immediately with activated charcoal, oral rehydration electrolytes, and specialized herbal broths. If medical attention is ever required, Ubud is home to reputable international clinics (such as Toya Medika and BIMC Ubud) with fluent English-speaking doctors located just 12 minutes away. You are never left to fend for yourself.
During the second week of our course, we take the entire cohort on an excursion to Tirta Empul, an eleventh-century holy water temple in Tampaksiring, for a traditional Balinese Melukat (water purification) ceremony. Standing waist-deep in cool spring water under eleven stone spouts as a Hindu priest chants mantras is frequently described by graduates as the spiritual highlight of their training.

