However, traditional Balinese Hinduism observes strict cultural protocols that solo travelers must understand and respect:

The Temple Dress Code: You cannot enter a sacred temple courtyard with exposed knees or shoulders. When visiting temples, you must wear a traditional Balinese sarong (kain) wrapped snugly around the waist and secured with a ceremonial temple sash (selendang). We provide ceremonial sarongs and teach you how to tie them properly.
The Menstruation Protocol: According to traditional Balinese Hindu philosophy, blood is considered spiritually charged, and entering sacred inner temple grounds or participating in water immersion during menstruation is considered spiritually disruptive to the temple's consecrated energy. Balinese women themselves adhere strictly to this custom. If your cycle coincides with our temple excursion day, do not feel discouraged—our Balinese teachers will guide you through an equally profound dry meditation and blessing in the outer temple garden.
Never Step on Canang Sari Offerings: Walking down any village street in Ubud, you will notice small square baskets woven from banana or palm leaves containing frangipani blossoms, rice, and burning incense sticks placed on the ground. These are Canang Sari, daily offerings made by Balinese women to thank the gods and appease spirits. Always step around them respectfully; never step directly over them.
The single greatest misconception about doing a YTT alone is the fear of loneliness. Most solo applicants imagine their first evening: eating dinner awkwardly in a corner, hoping someone talks to them.

That fear evaporates within forty-eight hours. Why? Because a 200-hour yoga teacher training is not a casual hotel vacation where guests sit poolside wearing headphones. You are engaged in an intense collective immersion:

Small cohort sisterhood of yoga teacher training students in Bali
An intimate cohort of 14–16 students breaking down philosophy, anatomy, and lifelong friendship.

Shared Vulnerability: During philosophy lectures on Patanjali's Yoga Sutras, non-violence (Ahimsa), and truthfulness (Satya), students openly share what brought them to Bali. Conversations quickly surpass superficial small talk about job titles and weather.
Physical Support in Adjustments: Learning how to safely assist someone into Virabhadrasana II or Matsyasana requires close communication, trust, and physical touch. You learn to listen to each other's bodies with deep empathy.
Small Cohort Cap (14–16 Students): Many commercial schools in Canggu cram 35 to 40 students into a cavernous shala where quiet students become invisible. We deliberately cap our student cohorts at 14 to 16 students. Every student is known by name, mentored closely by Master Vivek and Nyoman, and woven into the group dynamic.
By the time graduation day arrives, the women who arrived as anxious solo travelers from London, Sydney, Frankfurt, and New York leave with lifelong sisters, often traveling together across Bali and Lombok for weeks after the course finishes.

A true master school tells you what a course is not, so you make the right decision for your life:

This is not a party vacation: If your dream Bali trip involves sunset cocktails at beach clubs every night and sleeping in until 10:00 AM, our ashram will frustrate you. Wake-up bells ring at 5:45 AM, and quiet hours begin strictly at 9:30 PM.
Tropical insects exist: Ubud is a fertile highland rainforest. You will see geckos on the shala walls, hear frogs after evening rain, and encounter harmless garden spiders. Our villas are sprayed and cleaned daily, and beds have protective netting, but if you expect a sterile glass-and-chrome airport hotel, Ubud's raw nature may surprise you.
You will hit physical and emotional plateaus: Around Day 10 to Day 13, your muscles will feel fatigued, and subconscious emotions may surface. Growth requires moving through that discomfort. Our lead teachers Vivek and Nyoman are on campus every day to guide you through these moments with compassion.
Ubud has hundreds of quick, affordable laundry services (wash, dry, and fold for roughly $1.50 USD per kilogram). Do not overload your luggage with three weeks of clothes. Pack these essentials:

4–5 Sets of Lightweight Yoga Wear: Choose breathable, sweat-wicking synthetic blends rather than heavy thick cotton, which dries slowly in tropical humidity.
1 Lightweight Shawl or Cardigan: Ubud highland mornings at 6:00 AM can feel surprisingly cool (21°C / 70°F) before the sun burns through the mist.
Slip-on Sandals or Slides: In Bali, shoes are removed before entering any home, shala, temple, or dining hall. Laced sneakers become exhausting to tie twenty times a day.
Activated Charcoal Tablets & Electrolytes: Carry pure electrolyte powder to replenish minerals lost through daily physical sweating.
A Hardcover Journal: The mental realizations and teaching breakthroughs you experience over 21 days will fill pages you will re-read for decades.
